    "tags": ["HCI", "Photography", "Field data capture", "Telephony", "Still video"],
    "overview": "Announced August 26, 1988, the Nikon QV-1000C was Nikon's first filmless camera and a purpose-built professional still-video system. It recorded up to 50 frames onto a reusable 2-inch magnetic video floppy disk (same S-VHS format as camcorders) that could be ejected and re-shot thousands of times, eliminating film and processing. The camera captured a frame in about 1/1000th of a second and stored it as an NTSC analog signal that required no chemical development.\n\nFor transmission, the disk was inserted into the separate QT-1000C transmitter, which dialed out over a phone line via an acoustic coupler — the user clamped the handset onto the modem and the picture traveled through the ordinary telephone network as sound. This made the QV-1000C a complete remote-photojournalism chain: camera, disk, transmitter, acoustic coupler and phone. It previews today's instant-digital-photo paradigm, but with the eccentric embodied ritual of physically handing a disk from camera to transmitter and coupling it to a handset.",
    "deepDive": [
      {"heading": "The video floppy", "text": "Still images were stored on a reusable 2-inch magnetic disk holding up to 50 NTSC frames. The disk is the hinge of the system — it is physically ejected from the camera, carried to the transmitter, and reused indefinitely. The medium itself was the message: filmless, rewritable, and instantly transmittable."},
      {"heading": "The QT-1000C transmitter and acoustic coupler", "text": "The disk went into the QT-1000C transmission unit, which established a phone connection through an acoustic coupler — a cradle holding the telephone handset so audio tones carried the image data across the public switched telephone network. This is a visible, physical manifestation of early digital telephony."},
      {"heading": "A press workflow, not a camera", "text": "Nikon marketed the QV-1000C to newspapers and agencies as a total system for getting pictures from the field to the newsroom in minutes. It is better understood as an interaction chain than as a single camera: capture, carry the medium, dial, clamp, transmit."}
    ],
